    male|a||Nine lectures have each added a line to the same script; today it goes on one page, so that when it breaks you know which of its six blocks you are actually in.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Block one is setup: pick the datastore, version fifty-four, call it db, and open an empty dataset called persons.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||That colon fifty-four is a version pin, and in a year it is the difference between your colleague's number and yours.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Block two imports. Sex and birth year-month take no date, because neither of them moves.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Education, municipality and wage each carry one, because they are facts about a moment: schooling as of January twenty twenty, wage income on the last day of that year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Five imports in, and we still have not seen a single person. That is not an oversight, that is the design.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Block three builds what the datastore does not hold: birth arrives as one-nine-eight-five-zero-four, so integer-divide by a hundred and the cohort falls out.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Then the dummy — zero for everyone, one wherever the sex code is the string two.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Block four defines the population: everyone born in 1985, some fifty thousand people.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||And then positive wage income only — which quietly drops everyone with no earnings, and those people are not spread evenly across the two sexes.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||So the estimate you are about to see is conditional on working at all: three lines of code, and the hardest question in the paper.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Block five describes before it explains: how big the two groups are, and what wage income looks like.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Then the log, taken here and not earlier, because the logarithm of zero is where scripts go to die.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Block six is one line: log wage income on the female dummy and education.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a|grave|One coefficient, and everything above it is an argument about what that coefficient means — for a cohort like this, women's wage income on the order of twenty per cent lower.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Wage income, not hourly pay: part-time hours and months out of work are inside that number, so a gap measured here is a gap in earnings, not in rates.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Setup, import, new variables, population, describe, estimate — six blocks, always that order, because each one can only use what the one above it made.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Which is why a failure reported on the last line usually happened three blocks higher: the regression is just the first command that cared.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||One check before we open block one. Click the line that makes this script reproducible a year from now.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||The require line — the datastore pinned at version fifty-four, so a colleague runs against the same metadata you did.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||You can now read this page as six blocks instead of twenty-two lines — and say, for any error, which block owns it. Next time we open the first two.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Now that we have seen the whole script on one page, let us type the top of it properly: the setup block and the imports.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Line one, every single time: require no dot ssb dot fdb, colon fifty-four, as db.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||And fifty-four is a version of the datastore, pinned by hand — not the newest one, the one you chose.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||It is the least interesting character in the script, and the only one that can quietly ruin a year of work.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Then create-dataset persons: an empty table with a name, and every command after this line works on that table.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Lowercase noun, one unit of analysis — persons, because every row here is going to be one person born in 1985, and there are roughly fifty thousand of them.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Block two, and so far the dataset is a name with no columns at all.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||First import: BEFOLKNING underscore KJOENN, the population register''s sex variable, renamed to sex.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||No date on that line — sex does not change from one reference date to the next, so this is the dateless shape of import.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Same shape for the cohort variable: BEFOLKNING underscore FOEDSELS underscore AAR underscore MND, as birth.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||You type that name exactly once. That is the entire argument for the alias.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Now municipality, BEFOLKNING underscore KOMMNR underscore FORMELL — and here a date appears: the first of January 2022.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Because people move. Without a date the question which municipality has no answer; with one it means where they were formally registered on that day.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Education next, NUDB underscore BU, highest completed — dated shape again, and the same date, so all the columns are one consistent snapshot.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||And the outcome we actually care about: INNTEKT underscore WLONN, wage income, dated, as wage.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||This is where people trip. Wage income is an annual sum, so that date is not New Year's Day — it selects the period the date falls in, the income year 2022.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Write 2022-12-31 instead and you get exactly the same column — which is comforting once you know it, and baffling until then.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Five imports written. So what do we actually have in hand?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Here is the same setup trimmed to two variables, just so the output fits on the whiteboard.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||And one printing command, tabulate sex — because the only way to know an import landed is to ask the server to count it.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Two codes come back, one and two, men and women, with a count beside each — computed on microdata's synthetic data, never on anybody real.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||One row per person, one column per import — and it is the first import that fixed which rows exist at all.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Anyone that wage line does not cover comes back as sysmiss, a genuine missing value — never a quiet zero.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Quick check before we go on. Which of these two imports needs a date?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Wage income is measured over a period, so the date picks the income year; sex does not change, so it takes no date at all.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a|grave|So the top of every script does three things: it pins a datastore version, it names one table with one unit of analysis, and then — one date at a time — it decides what as of when means for each column.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Next part: from that imported population to the regression — keeping the 1985 cohort, and measuring the gap itself.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Now that we have the require line and the imports, everything left is what decides what the final number MEANS — one coefficient, and twenty lines of choices standing behind it.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Birth month arrives as text — one nine eight five zero four — so destring makes it a number, and integer division by a hundred leaves the birth year.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||And a single line defines the whole study population: keep if cohort equals 1985. Five and a half million people become some sixty thousand.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Put it here, before anything is computed, and the other cohorts never get processed. The server bills you in patience rather than kroner.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Education is a six-digit NUS code whose first digit is the level, so dividing by a hundred thousand and taking the integer keeps exactly that digit.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Recode then folds those levels into three: compulsory, upper secondary, higher education.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Sex is a string code, so the dummy is built by hand — zero for everyone, then one wherever the code is two.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||And the cross-tab from lecture seven, checking that no female-by-education cell is empty, survives as a comment — which is where good intentions live.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Now the part that trips people up. Right at the top, before persons exists, a second dataset: muni, with just municipality and wage.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Collapse averages wage inside each municipality, and the unit of analysis changes under you — sixty thousand people become about three hundred and fifty rows.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||The by variable IS the new unit. Nothing in the dataset is a person any more.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Back in persons, merge pulls that average in on kommune, so every person in Bergen now carries Bergen's mean wage as a column of their own.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Worth stopping on: that mean is computed from the same people we are about to explain. Manski called it the reflection problem — the group average and the individual are not independent evidence.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||So, the last line: wage income on the female dummy, education level, and the municipal mean.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||A coefficient table comes back — one row per variable, a standard error beside it, and the row everyone will quote.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Kroner per year: the average difference between women and men born in 1985, with education level and municipal mean held fixed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a|grave|Not the effect of being a woman. Hours, part-time work, occupation and career breaks are all still inside that number — a conditional average difference, nothing more.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||And this table is all you ever see: no residuals, no individuals, no scatter of the people behind the line.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Quick check before we hand this script to a colleague. What does the coefficient on female in this regression measure?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||It is a conditional average difference — the gap left after education level and municipal mean are held fixed, with hours and occupation still inside it.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a|grave|Twenty-two lines: require, import, destring, generate, keep, recode, collapse, merge, regress. Only the last one mentions the gender gap — and every line above it decided which gap you measured.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Now that we have seen the whole script run from require all the way to the regression, let us watch it fail — because it will.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||And where it fails matters: not on your laptop, but over here, behind the wall, where one row is one person you will never see.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||You send the entire script, every time, from line one — nothing sits there remembering your variables between runs.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||And exactly two kinds of thing come back: a table of numbers, or a message.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||That message is your entire debugger. No browsing rows, no printing the first five cases — one line of text, and your own script.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||So here are the four messages you will actually meet, on the same script as always: the 1985 cohort and their wages.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||The wage import, typed from memory as inntekt-loenn.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Unknown variable. No suggestion, no near-match, no did-you-mean.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||It is INNTEKT underscore W-LONN, W for wage — and that name is checked against the metadata catalogue before a single row is touched, so a typo costs you a second rather than a place in the queue.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Second message, and it is the one you get while tidying up: require, then straight into the import.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||No dataset in memory — which sounds like the server forgot something, and means you did.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||An import needs somewhere to land; delete create-dataset and every import below it is homeless.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Those two were about names and order. The next two are about what a row actually is.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||From lecture four: we collapse wages to municipality means, then try to merge them back onto our people.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Different units of analysis — municipality against person.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||After the collapse a row is a municipality: three hundred and fifty-odd rows where fifty thousand people stood, and nobody here will quietly spread an average back over individuals for you.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Then, keeping the women in the cohort: sex equals two.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Type mismatch. KJOENN is a coded string, not a count — the code for a woman is the character two, not the number.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Quote it and the line runs. Two characters, forty minutes. Same family as writing wage equals dot when what the language wants is sysmiss.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Four messages, two families. Names and state, caught up front — against the catalogue, and against your script's own order.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Nothing has run yet when these two fire; they are about your text, not your data.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Shape and type are caught while it runs, against the structure of the dataset you yourself built two lines earlier.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a|grave|And every one of them talks about your script or the metadata. Not one names a person, a row, or a value.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||That is the same disclosure rule that censored your output in lecture eight, reaching into the error messages — a message that quoted a value would itself be a small leak.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||One quick check before we hand this over. Which message can microdata.no never send you?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||No message can quote a row — the errors describe your script and the metadata, never the data behind the wall.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||So debugging here is never reading the data — it is reading your script against the metadata, and against the shape of what you built. Which leaves next time''s question: which line was it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Now that we can read what the error says, the harder question: the message names the last line, and the last line is almost never the culprit.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Here is the skeleton of our gender-gap script — sex, birth month and wage income, imported onto one dataset.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Then the population block: keep the women, and drop anyone with no wage record at all.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||And the payoff line — a summarize here, the regression with education and municipality in the full script.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||You run it, the server says no observations, and it points here — at the one line that is definitely innocent.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||So instead of guessing, we put checkpoints in: commented summarizes, one per block, switched on when you need them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Switch on check A, run only that far, and you see the dataset straight after import.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||A count, a mean, a spread. The numbers hardly matter — what matters is that there are any.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||This bites harder here than in Stata: you cannot browse a single row, so an aggregate like this is the only window you have into your own data.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Check B, after the population block, says how many survived it — about twenty-five thousand women from the eighty-five cohort, or nobody at all.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Block by block is fine on ten lines. Our real script is forty, and that is forty runs.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Forty runs and a lunch break.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||So halve it instead: one check in the middle, right after the imports, and run only that much.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Healthy there, and the bug is below it; broken, and it is above. Either way half the script stops being your problem.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Halve what remains, and again — forty lines takes six runs, not forty, because you are counting in log two of the length.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||Same trick as git bisect, which is how kernel developers find one bad commit among thousands.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||And the sixth run lands here. The version that broke said keep if sysmiss wage — keep exactly the people who have no wage.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b|grave|Twenty-five thousand women in, zero rows out, and the regression four blocks later never had a chance.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||The message named the end of the file. The damage was done in the middle of it.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||The fix is one word: drop the missing, do not keep them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Leave the checkpoints in the file afterwards — a commented-out summarize costs nothing and saves the next afternoon.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a|grave|One bad line never means a broken script. It means you were not printing the state often enough to see where it stopped being true.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Now that we can isolate a failing line, the last question is the one a referee asks: if someone reruns this script next spring, do they get your twelve log points back?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||And the answer hides in the line everybody types and nobody reads — require, no dot ssb dot fdb, colon fifty-four.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Fifty-four is a version of the datastore: the registers exactly as they stood when that version was cut.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||So next spring, when the store has moved on to fifty-seven?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Then you get fifty-seven's data — corrected 2022 wage figures, late address registrations — and the coefficient moves in the third decimal.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Which is what survey people find hardest here: a register is not a photograph of a year, it is a ledger that keeps being corrected long after the year is over.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Version pinned. Now the variable names, which are half the documentation — BEFOLKNING underscore KJOENN is one specific coding, not just "sex".:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||And every time-dependent import carries its date, because wage income at the end of 2022 is a different variable from wage income at the end of 2021.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Tell a colleague only "wage income for the 1985 cohort" and they will reproduce something. Just not this.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Then who counts: born in 1985, and earning something that year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||Two lines with an argument buried in them — drop the zero-earners and you have dropped much of the population the gap is about.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||The last block is the estimate: a female indicator, log wage, and the regression we have been building since lecture one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||About twelve log points — one cohort, one year, one version of the registers.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b||Which is why the top line is not courtesy: version, cohort, and the date the output was pulled.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|a||That pull date earns its place, because censoring applies to the run, not the script — the same command can come back suppressed on a day a cell happens to be too small.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    male|b||And nobody can send you the data file. The script is the only artefact that travels, which does simplify version control.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|a||Four lines carry the whole result: the version, the variable with its date, the population rule, and the estimator. Everything else is plumbing.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'male|b|grave|So the series ends where it began: in microdata the script is not documentation of the analysis, it is the analysis — pin the version, date the variables, say who is in, and the twelve log points come back a year from now.':
      mp3: 
